/*!
    \example simple_service
    \title Simple Service
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \meta sortkey {30}
    \brief Answering std_srvs/SetBool requests from QML with a single,
    mostly declarative service server.

    \c{Simple Service} shows the server side of a ROS 2 service in QML: it wraps
    one \l{Qt ROS2 std_srvs QML Types}{SetBoolServiceServer} in a \l{Qt ROS2 Core
    QML Types}{Node} and switches a "lamp" on or off whenever a
    \c std_srvs/SetBool request arrives — from the \l{Simple Service Client}
    example, from \c{ros2 service call}, or from any other ROS 2 node. To make
    failure handling honest, the bulb burns out after ten switching cycles and
    the service starts reporting \c{success: false} until the bulb is replaced.

    \image simple_service/simple_service.png "The Simple Service window: an editable topic, the lamp that requests switch on and off, bulb wear, a deferred-reply switch, and the last request."

    \section1 Running the Example

    \list 1
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment, then launch Qt Creator from the same
            shell so it can find the generated QML modules.
        \li Open \c examples/simple_service/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator, then
            build and run.
        \li Call the service from a separate, ROS-sourced terminal:
            \badcode
            ros2 service call /simple_service_lamp std_srvs/srv/SetBool "{data: true}"
            \endcode
    \endlist

    Pair this example with \l{Simple Service Client} to see both ends of a
    service in two Qt windows.

    \section1 A Declarative Service Server

    Like publishers and subscribers, a service server is declared as a child of
    a \c Node; the node owns the underlying \c rclcpp service and tears it down
    automatically. In the simplest style there is no callback at all: the
    server's \c response property is a binding, and each incoming request is
    answered with its current value. The incoming payload is available as the
    read-only \c request property:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.StdSrvs

    Node {
        id: rosNode
        nodeName: "simple_service_node"

        SetBoolServiceServer {
            id: lampService
            topic: topicField.text

            onRequestReceived: request => ++root.cycles

            response: root.burnedOut
                ? ({ success: false, message: "the bulb is burned out" })
                : ({ success: true, message: `lamp is now ${lampService.request ? "on" : "off"}` })
        }
    }
    \endqml

    The work splits along QML's usual grain: derived state lives in bindings
    (the lamp color binds to \c{lampService.request}), per-request effects live
    in the \c onRequestReceived signal handler (counting switching cycles), and
    the response — including failure — is a conditional expression. The
    evaluation order is guaranteed: \c request updates first, then
    \c requestReceived fires, and only then is \c response read and sent. That
    is why the very request that burns out the bulb already receives the
    failure answer.

    For \c std_srvs/SetBool the request payload is a single \c bool, so
    \c request is a plain boolean. Services with multi-field requests receive
    the generated value type instead, with the same \c camelCase field access
    as messages. The response works symmetrically: a plain JavaScript object
    with the response fields (here \c success and \c message) converts to the
    \c SetBool response automatically.

    \section1 Failure Is In-Band

    ROS 2 services have no separate error channel — a server that cannot
    comply still answers, encoding failure in the response. That is exactly
    why \c std_srvs types carry \c success and \c message fields, and why
    failure needs no special API here: it is just another branch of the
    \c response binding.

    \section1 Handlers and Deferred Replies

    When producing the response requires running code per request — or when
    honoring the request takes time — set the
    \l {ServiceServerBase::handler}{handler} property instead; a callable
    handler takes precedence over the \c response property. The handler may
    return the response value directly, or return \c undefined and call
    \c{reply.send()} later. The example's \e{Delay the response} switch
    demonstrates the precedence rule by binding the handler property itself:

    \qml
    handler: delaySwitch.checked
        ? (request, reply) => {
              replyTimer.pendingReplies.push(reply)
              replyTimer.restart()
          }
        : undefined
    \endqml

    \qml
    Timer {
        id: replyTimer
        interval: 1000
        property var pendingReplies: []
        onTriggered: {
            for (const reply of pendingReplies)
                reply.send(lampService.response)   // same declarative value, later
            pendingReplies = []
        }
    }
    \endqml

    With the switch on, callers observe the answer arriving one second late —
    while the server window stays fully responsive and keeps accepting further
    requests: the handler runs on the GUI thread and the ROS executor is never
    blocked, even while replies are pending.

    If neither a handler nor a \c response binding is set (or the handler
    throws), the server sends a default-constructed response so that callers
    never wait forever.

    \sa {Simple Service Client}, {Qt ROS2 std_srvs QML Types}
*/
